\section RoboticsIntro Introduction

This plug-in simulates a robot loaded from a URDF or MJCF file in a \ref PhysicsEngine simulation. The robot's joints are
driven by position servos, its tool can be placed with inverse kinematics, and the force on its tool can be read. The robot
is simulated, not animated: it follows its servos, what it touches pushes back, and an overloaded or poorly placed arm is
pushed off its target.

\section RoboticsUse Using the plug-in

\code{.cpp}
    Context context;
    PhysicsEngine engine(&context);

    Robot robot(&engine, "arm.urdf", "robot");
    robot.addJointServo("shoulder", 2000.f, 400.f, 0.f, 2.f);
    robot.addJointServo("elbow", 1000.f, 200.f, 0.f, 1.f);
    robot.addJointServo("wrist", 200.f, 30.f, 0.f, 0.2f);
    robot.setTool("tool", make_vec3(0.2f, 0, 0), make_vec3(1, 0, 0));

    engine.initialize();
    robot.initialize();

    RobotInverseKinematicsResult pose = robot.solveInverseKinematics(make_vec3(0.8f, 0, 1), make_vec3(1, 0, 0));
    if (pose.converged) {
        robot.setJointTargets(pose.joint_positions);
    }
    engine.step(1000);
    helios::vec3 tool_force = robot.getToolForce();
\endcode

The order matters: the robot is described before PhysicsEngine::initialize(), and Robot::initialize() connects it to the compiled model afterward.
Joint positions are in degrees for hinges and meters for slides. See <tt>samples/robotics_pruning</tt> for a complete example, in which a robot pushes into a
grapevine shoot, measures the force, and cuts it.

\section RoboticsModels Robot models

The model's geometry collides with articulations such as plants, but not with itself, so it is the job of whatever plans the motion to keep the arm from folding through itself.
In a URDF file, include <tt>&lt;mujoco&gt;&lt;compiler fusestatic="false" discardvisual="true"/&gt;&lt;/mujoco&gt;</tt> so that bodies joined by fixed joints are kept, and give every link an inertial with a positive mass.

\section RoboticsServos Choosing servo gains

A servo is only stable when its damping is small compared with the inertia it drives: damping * timestep / (link inertia + armature) should be well below one. Light links near the tool
would need very low damping to meet this, but on a real robot the motor's inertia, reflected through its gearing, is usually far larger than theirs. Set it with the armature argument of Robot::addJointServo().
An unstable servo shows up as large oscillations of the arm or an error from PhysicsEngine::step().

\section RoboticsForce Reading force

Robot::getToolForce() sums the contact forces on the tool and its descendants. It is zero when nothing touches the tool, and needs no taring. It is what an ideal wrist sensor would read once the tool's weight and inertia are removed, with no noise or bandwidth limit.
Use it rather than PhysicsEngine::addForceTorqueSensor() while the arm is moving, since that sensor's reading is only reliable at rest.
A ContactSensor on the robot (see \ref PhysicsEngineContactSensor) splits the contact force by what the robot touches, such as wood, fruit, or leaves.

\section RoboticsComponents Actions, end effectors, and safety

These parts of a robot task run in C++ and are configured once, so that a task need not write them itself. Each is created after Robot::initialize(), and keeps state per simulation instance.
Each is a RobotComponent, which a ControlLoop calls at every physics step, and the actions are also RobotAction objects, which a controller commands with a block of numbers each control step.

- ToolVelocityAction moves the tool point at a commanded Cartesian velocity: each control step it moves a target, solves inverse kinematics for it, and sets the joint targets. The target may lead the tool by at most a set distance,
  so that it does not run away while the arm is blocked. The tool axis stays along a fixed direction, or with ToolVelocityAction::setToolAxisAwayFromPivot() points horizontally away from the turret an arm turns on.
- VacuumCup is an ideal suction cup on a robot body, which seals on a spherical target such as a fruit only when its whole lip is on it, holds the target seated in its mouth with springs, and reports when the pull, the load across the
  mouth, or the moment that peels it pass what the seal holds (VacuumCupParameters). In a ControlLoop it lets go of the target when the seal breaks. VacuumCupGripAction commands it.
- ProtectiveStop trips when the contact force on the robot measured by a ContactSensor, leaving out partners such as the target it is meant to touch, passes a limit. It is checked at every physics step, so that a peak
  between control steps is not missed.
- JointPositionAction sets joint targets from commands scaled to the joints' ranges, and JointVelocityAction moves them at commanded speeds, for robots such as gantries driven joint by joint.

\code{.cpp}
    ContactSensor contact(&engine, "robot/base_link");
    ProtectiveStop stop(&contact, 50.f, {"target"});
    VacuumCup cup(&robot, "tool", make_vec3(0.32f, 0, 0), make_vec3(1, 0, 0));
    ToolVelocityAction move(&robot, 0.3f, 0.08f);

    VacuumCupGripAction grip(&cup);
    ControlLoop loop(&engine, 20); // 20 physics steps per control step
    loop.addComponent(&cup);
    loop.addComponent(&stop);
    loop.addComponent(&move);

    // Each episode
    loop.resetInstance(instance);
    engine.setDetachForce(plant, fruit_link, 20.f, instance); // the stem fails at 20 N
    cup.setTarget(plant, fruit_link, instance);
    contact.setTargetLink(plant, fruit_link, instance);
    robot.setJointPositions(start_pose, instance);

    // Each control step: actions, then the physics steps, which end early if the fruit comes off, the seal breaks, or the stop trips
    move.applyAction(velocity_command, loop.getControlPeriod(), instance);
    grip.applyAction(grip_command, loop.getControlPeriod(), instance);
    ControlStepResult result = loop.step(instance);
\endcode

\section RoboticsParallel Many scenes and copies

ParallelRobotSimulations holds the scenes a task is trained on, such as several trees, each a PhysicsEngine with its ControlLoop and actions, and every instance of each as a copy. ParallelRobotSimulations::step() takes the
actions of every copy in one array and runs a control step of each on a pool of threads kept for its life. ParallelRobotSimulations::forEachCopy() and ParallelRobotSimulations::gather() run anything else over all copies on
the same threads, such as observations, rewards, and resets, so that a task's code for one copy is all it has to write. The scenes are built and owned by the task.

<tt>samples/robotics_apple_harvest</tt> builds its task from these, with \ref NearestPlantOrgan for the wood near the cup.

\section RoboticsCamera Cameras

With the visualizer plug-in in the project's list of plug-ins, RobotCamera renders a scene as it stands in one simulation instance, from a camera placed in the world (RobotCamera::setPose()) or mounted on a body such as a
robot's wrist (RobotCamera::mountOnBody()). Each frame gives a color image, the depth of each pixel, and the Context primitive and object seen in each pixel, so a task can use images as observations or measure how much of a
fruit is visible. Without the visualizer plug-in, the rest of the robotics plug-in builds and runs without OpenGL.

OpenGL is tied to a thread, so construct the camera and draw on the thread that will render. RobotCamera::prepare() needs no OpenGL, so the cameras of different scenes can prepare in parallel and then draw one after another
(RobotCamera::draw()). Use one camera per Context: each clears the Context's geometry flags after a frame, so that the next frame redraws only what moved. To draw a movie from recorded poses, show them with
PhysicsEngine::showDisplayBodyPoses() and render the instance.

\section RoboticsResets Home pose and parallel instances

Robot::setHomePose() sets the pose the robot starts from. Every method that reads or commands the robot takes a simulation instance, so one Robot drives all instances created with PhysicsEngine::createInstance().
Use Robot::resetInstance() to reset one, which restores the home pose and the servo targets. PhysicsEngine::resetInstance() returns the servo targets to zero. Robot::setJointPositions() places the joints directly.

Instances can be simulated from separate threads, each one on a single thread at a time. Robot::solveInverseKinematics() can run on them at the same time, after a call to Robot::prepareInstances() once all instances exist.
See <tt>samples/robotics_apple_harvest</tt>, which trains a policy this way and has a camera on the wrist of the robot, with one Visualizer per tree. When rendering from a moving camera every frame, hide the Visualizer's navigation gizmo, which is rebuilt whenever the camera moves.

*/
